ed items in an SSL context and clear memory\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      SSL context\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_free(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl);\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_SSL_CONTEXT_SERIALIZATION)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Save an active connection as serialized data in a buffer.\0A *                 This allows the freeing or re-using of the SSL context\0A *                 while still picking up the connection later in a way that\0A *                 it entirely transparent to the peer.\0A *\0A * \5Csee            mbedtls_ssl_context_load()\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           The serialized data only contains the data that is\0A *                 necessary to resume the connection: negotiated protocol\0A *                 options, session identifier, keys, etc.\0A *                 Loading a saved SSL context does not restore settings and\0A *                 state related to how the application accesses the context,\0A *                 such as configured callback functions, user data, pending\0A *                 incoming or outgoing data, etc.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This feature is currently only available under certain\0A *                 conditions, see the documentation of the return value\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_BAD_INPUT_DATA for details.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           When this function succeeds, it calls\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_session_reset() on \5Cp ssl which as a result is\0A *                 no longer associated with the connection that has been\0A *                 serialized. This avoids creating copies of the connection\0A *                 state. You're then free to either re-use the context\0A *                 structure for a different connection, or call\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_free() on it. See the documentation of\0A *                 mbedtls_ssl_session_reset() for more details.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ssl      The SSL context to save. On success, it is no longer\0A *                 associated with the connection that