or unsupported body framing also\0A/// receives 400 before reading content or dispatching the handler. Repeated\0A/// or comma-separated Content-Length values must all agree and use decimal\0A/// digits within Int range. Requests without framing have an empty body;\0A/// bytes beyond a declared length are excluded from that request's body.\0A/// HTTP/1.1 Expect: 100-continue is acknowledged before waiting for content;\0A/// unsupported expectations receive 417. HTTP/1.0 expectations are ignored.\0A/// HTTP/1.0 may omit Host. Present Host values use URI host\0A/// syntax, including empty values, IP literals, and optional decimal ports.\0A/// Real, useful on its own\0A/// (a one-shot server, or a test fixture — this is what THIS module's\0A/// own tests exercise directly), not just a building block for `http_\0A/// serve` below.\0Apub let serve_once (port: Int) (handler: (Request) -> Response): Result[Unit, String] =\0A    match Net.listen_on(port) {\0A        Err(e) => Err(e),\0A        Ok(server) => match Net.accept(server) {\0A            Err(e) => { Net.close(server); Err(e) },\0A            Ok(conn) => {\0A                let result = http_handle_connection(conn, handler);\0A                Net.close(conn);\0A                Net.close(server);\0A                result\0A            },\0A        },\0A    }\0A\0A/// The REAL long-running server: accept, hand the connection off to its\0A/// own task, repeat — forever. Concurrent by design: `spawn` puts each\0A/// connection's `http_handle_connection`/close on its own real OS\0A/// thread (see DESIGN.md's Concurrency section — \22OS threads first\22)\0A/// so one slow client (a slow body, a slow handler) can't stall every\0A/// OTHER connection behind it the way a single sequential accept-loop\0A/// would. A single misbehaving/erroring connection does NOT bring the\0A/// server down (the spawned task's `Result` is deliberately never\0A/// joined, let alone matched — a bare discarded statement, same idiom\0A/// `print